1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,635
. This recording discusses

2
00:00:02,635 --> 00:00:06,205
embedded testing part 4 of
our embedded test series.

3
00:00:06,205 --> 00:00:09,625
This is going to go
over TDD and BDD,

4
00:00:09,625 --> 00:00:12,890
test-driven development and
behavior-driven development.

5
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:15,475
We're going to introduce
test-driven development

6
00:00:15,475 --> 00:00:17,650
and introduce
behavior-driven development,

7
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:18,940
just give you an overview of

8
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:21,650
these different testing tools.

9
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:26,515
Test-driven development
is a common paradigm

10
00:00:26,515 --> 00:00:28,735
that can be used when
testing software.

11
00:00:28,735 --> 00:00:30,760
The idea is to write
requirements as

12
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:33,425
test case as part of
the test process.

13
00:00:33,425 --> 00:00:35,925
You start out with a
set of requirements,

14
00:00:35,925 --> 00:00:38,340
you write test case to
those requirements,

15
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:40,495
you run the test case,
and the test case

16
00:00:40,495 --> 00:00:43,645
obviously fails because you
haven't written software yet.

17
00:00:43,645 --> 00:00:46,040
You start out with
a set of failing

18
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:47,270
test cases that describe

19
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:49,210
the requirements for
the application.

20
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:52,080
You then write the
software and you

21
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,540
iterate on writing the software

22
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,690
until the test cases
that you wrote pass.

23
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:57,510
This is a very useful way.

24
00:00:57,510 --> 00:00:59,660
It's often used in what's
called pair programming,

25
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:01,700
where you can have a
different developer

26
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:02,720
write the test case and the

27
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:03,980
developer that writes the code,

28
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:07,770
and work on the two
things in parallel.

29
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:12,900
A few pitfalls
with this approach

30
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:14,600
: Usually it's going to be

31
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:15,950
a developer that's writing

32
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:17,690
the tests for the requirements,

33
00:01:17,690 --> 00:01:19,310
and in order for
the developer to

34
00:01:19,310 --> 00:01:20,645
write test for the requirements,

35
00:01:20,645 --> 00:01:22,810
they need to understand
the requirements.

36
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:24,140
But often the requirements

37
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,655
aren't coming from
the developer,

38
00:01:25,655 --> 00:01:30,215
so the success of
this whole paradigm

39
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:31,790
requires that the developer

40
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:36,220
understands and interprets
the requirements properly.

41
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:38,870
There's a chance that if the
developer doesn't understand

42
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:41,300
and interpret the
requirements properly,

43
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:43,550
then both the test and
the software could be

44
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:46,900
implemented wrong causing
success in a test case,

45
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:49,250
but ultimately making the
application not work the

46
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:53,850
way it was designed to work
from a business perspective.

47
00:01:53,990 --> 00:01:57,410
This is where behavior-driven
development is

48
00:01:57,410 --> 00:02:00,515
an interesting idea about

49
00:02:00,515 --> 00:02:03,080
how to use test-driven
development,

50
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,050
but in a slightly different way.

51
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:07,490
The different way really

52
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:09,620
has to do with how the
tests are defined.

53
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:12,260
Rather than having a test
case that's written in

54
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:13,400
a programming language that

55
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:15,945
only a developer can really
read and understand,

56
00:02:15,945 --> 00:02:18,350
the idea behind behavior-driven
development is to write

57
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:20,060
a test case in a
natural language

58
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:21,860
that non-programmers can read.

59
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:27,230
This is often referred to in
BDD parlance as a scenario.

60
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:29,570
The scenario just
describes the purpose and

61
00:02:29,570 --> 00:02:31,700
the benefit of the
code that you're

62
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:33,950
implementing for the
application and doesn't

63
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:36,770
really get into the technical
details about how it works.

64
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:39,350
There's a special
Gherkin language syntax

65
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:41,440
that you can use for this.

66
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:45,245
Here's an example of
the difference between

67
00:02:45,245 --> 00:02:49,490
an implementation
and an interaction

68
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:51,200
that could happen between,

69
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,075
for instance, a product manager

70
00:02:53,075 --> 00:02:54,680
who is setting requirements for

71
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:57,080
the product and developers

72
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:58,550
that are implementing
different portions

73
00:02:58,550 --> 00:03:00,410
of the development.

74
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:03,790
You can see here the
product manager saying,

75
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:06,500
we want to encourage new
users to buy in our shop.

76
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:09,445
We'll offer 10 percent
discount for the first order,

77
00:03:09,445 --> 00:03:12,170
and the developers
turning this into code.

78
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:15,470
They're thinking about the
calculate discount function

79
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:18,020
that's going to calculate
the discount on the order.

80
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:20,810
They're thinking about
the implementation

81
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:23,555
of the front end,

82
00:03:23,555 --> 00:03:24,870
the web UI,

83
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:29,155
that's going to do the search
and add to cart functions.

84
00:03:29,155 --> 00:03:33,530
You can see that this
part probably requires

85
00:03:33,530 --> 00:03:36,170
someone with
engineering background

86
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:38,480
to really understand
and make sense of,

87
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:43,150
whereas these requirements
can be understood by anyone.

88
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:46,205
The idea is to try to
make the test case

89
00:03:46,205 --> 00:03:49,790
more representative of what
the true requirements are.

90
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:52,640
What if instead of
thinking like this about

91
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:54,680
the test implementation and

92
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:56,800
the actual code implementation,

93
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,100
product manager could
make the same statement,

94
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:03,290
but then the developers could
think of the test case and

95
00:04:03,290 --> 00:04:05,725
the implementation
in the sense of just

96
00:04:05,725 --> 00:04:09,130
common Given-When-Then
type of syntax.

97
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:11,180
You could actually write
a test case that says,

98
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:13,040
given that the user
hasn't ordered,

99
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:16,280
when the user adds a book
with whatever the price is,

100
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:19,790
then the shopping cart
sub-total is whatever.

101
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:25,220
The difference here being
that the product manager,

102
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:27,175
even if they don't have an
engineering background,

103
00:04:27,175 --> 00:04:29,440
they can look through
these test cases.

104
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:32,425
If you picture a whole bank
of test cases like this,

105
00:04:32,425 --> 00:04:34,820
the product manager
could help analyze, "Oh,

106
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:37,550
there's one other requirement
that I forgot to mention.

107
00:04:37,550 --> 00:04:40,240
You need to make sure you
cover this particular case."

108
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:42,800
They can look for
gaps like that,

109
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:44,030
but they wouldn't
be able to do if it

110
00:04:44,030 --> 00:04:46,410
was all implemented in code.

111
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:48,830
There are several
different tools you can

112
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:51,125
use for behavior-driven
development.

113
00:04:51,125 --> 00:04:54,020
If you're using Python
development environment,

114
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:57,935
there's a tool called behave
that can be used for this.

115
00:04:57,935 --> 00:05:00,020
Ruby programming
language also has

116
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:01,705
a very popular one
called Cucumber.

117
00:05:01,705 --> 00:05:03,530
You'll probably see a
lot about Cucumber if

118
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:06,790
you look up behavior-driven
development paradigms.

119
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:09,380
The snapshot that I have

120
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:12,500
below is an example of a
behave implementation.

121
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:16,535
This would be a Python
implementation of BDD.

122
00:05:16,535 --> 00:05:19,150
You can see these scenarios,

123
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:23,145
the overview of the tests
that it's covering,

124
00:05:23,145 --> 00:05:25,845
and then a given,
and then statement.

125
00:05:25,845 --> 00:05:28,965
This is really what's
driving the test case.

126
00:05:28,965 --> 00:05:30,810
Given I search for
a valid account,

127
00:05:30,810 --> 00:05:33,130
then I will see account details.

128
00:05:33,130 --> 00:05:35,210
How this is actually implemented

129
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:37,330
from a programmer perspective,

130
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:39,825
these given and then steps,

131
00:05:39,825 --> 00:05:45,270
you can map the English strings
possibly with variables.

132
00:05:45,270 --> 00:05:47,010
You can get these more complex.

133
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:49,910
You can map these directly
into step functions that are

134
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:51,290
implemented in Python and that

135
00:05:51,290 --> 00:05:52,945
are specific to the application.

136
00:05:52,945 --> 00:05:55,335
But the idea is,
from a high level,

137
00:05:55,335 --> 00:05:57,750
these can be described in

138
00:05:57,750 --> 00:06:01,180
text like this that
anyone can understand.